---
T1.8 (#1021): the closing half of `positive/1021_refined_sugar_equivalence_cross_context`.
That fixture pins that the fold *collapses* — `NonEmpty` and `MinLength(1)` are
the same refinement, so the boundary opens. This one pins that it collapses
*exactly*: the fold is a rewrite of one spelling into another, not a subsumption
rule, so a strictly tighter callee refinement must still fail closed.

The callee wants `String where MinLength(2)`; the caller offers
`String where NonEmpty`, which canonicalises to `MinLength(1)`. A one-character
token satisfies the caller and violates the callee, so `refinements_match` must
reject it — as `structurally_compare_named`'s exact-match check does today. If
someone later widens the fold into "`MinLength(2)` implies `MinLength(1)`, so
admit it", this fixture is what stops it.
---
context boundary.consumer

consumes boundary.provider as Provider

type LocalToken = String where NonEmpty

service submit {
  on call(value: LocalToken) -> Effect[Result[Int, Int]] {
    let _ <- Provider.verify(value)
    Effect.pure(Ok(0))
  }
}
